    "title": "Centerstone raising 988 crisis lifeline awareness with help of schools, businesses - NewsBreak",
    "text": "By Sara Smith ,\n41 minutes ago\nNASHVILLE, Tenn. (WKRN) — Mental health experts at Centerstone are working to raise awareness of the 988 crisis lifeline, and they’re partnering with local businesses and schools to do it.\nYou could now be seeing 988 signs around places like schools, grocery stores and doctor offices.\n“Anywhere where there are people, we want signs to normalize 988 when you’re struggling,” Valerie Klein with Centerstone said.\n📧 Have breaking news come to you: Subscribe to News 2 email alerts →\nIt’s a push by the state and Centerstone to remind people that 988, the three-digit phone number, is there for tough times.\n“They’ll reach someone who provides a caring, non-judgemental space for them to talk through whatever crisis they’re experiencing,” Klein said.\nClarksville-Montgomery County Schools was one of the first organizations that said yes to the campaign.\nMental health experts said that teen and young adult suicide rates are up.\nSchool officials said they’re thankful for the partnership with Centerstone.\n⏩ Read today’s top stories on wkrn.com\nThe signs are set to be installed soon.\nThe campaign is being paid for by the Mental Health and Substance Abuse Services.\nCenterstone said to contact them if you want 988 signs.\nThe National Suicide Prevention Lifeline is a prevention network of 161 crisis centers that provides a 24/7, toll-free hotline available to anyone in suicidal crisis or emotional distress. If you or someone you know needs support now, call or text 988 or chat at 988lifeline.org.\nCopyright 2025 Nexstar Media, Inc. All rights reserved. This material may not be published, broadcast, rewritten, or redistributed. For the latest news, weather, sports, and streaming video, head to WKRN News 2.",
